[48:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, if we search Septa B, so notice there are actually three processes here. Uh that this is something very very proud of. Uh I made this program basically crash'

[48:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'immune in that uh even if it does crash, everything\'s fine. Um So, this is the main Septa B process. This is the process doing all the audio'

[48:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'processing. So, the part that\'s actually doing the audio synthesis and outputting the audio is entirely separate from what we you\'re interacting with here. And because of that, if you have, say, a VST plugin and that'

[48:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'VST crashes, what most programs do is they sandbox the VST. And this usually isn\'t real-time safe and it can add tons of like CPU crackles and it\'s just really slow and horrible and miserable'

[48:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and it\'s really not a good system. But they do that so that if the VST crashes, it\'s in its own process, so it doesn\'t crash the program along with it. But my idea was to instead have a separate process for synthesizing the'

[49:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'audio. So So, like this program I\'m working with right here, this doesn\'t synthesize anything. It is giving instructions to an entirely separate program. It\'s very very cool. So, then if uh So, then I can run those VSTs and'

[49:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'such in process. So, if somebody writes a bad plugin and it crashes all the time, it won\'t crash Septa B. It will only crash the audio process. And when that'

[49:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'happens, a button will appear at the top saying, "Hey, something tried to crash the program, but we said no. Uh click here to restart the thing." And it\'ll just resurrect to audio process'

[49:49] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The system monitor remains open, showing the "septabee" processes.
AUDIO: 'like nothing even happened. But as you saw, this program crashed earlier. What\'s happening there is very, very cool. I came up with this idea just just a few weeks ago, and it works so well.'

[50:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Uh we have this watchdog process here. Its main purpose is to check uh is to make sure these programs close properly if the program closes down weirdly or if it crashes or whatever.'

[50:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'But what\'s also happening is whenever you make a change in this program, it slowly drip feeds save data over to this other process, and it only does a'

[50:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'little bit at a time, so the CPU usage in the GUI thread is down to like 2% or 3%. It uses barely anything. And eventually it\'ll kind of catch up to'

[50:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you, and every time it manages to get a full snapshot of what your project currently is, it makes a backup of it. And it it even keeps that in memory. So if this crashes,'

[50:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it can just resurrect the program and have exactly the same save data that you just barely had seconds before, 100% identical.'

[51:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Uh so even if uh there\'s a power and uh not a power outage cuz that would kill the watchdog, too. Though though the backup would still be there. Um I don\'t really have a way to access that'

[51:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'currently, do I? I guess not. I\'ll I\'ll be doing that eventually. But but regardless, as you saw, it could just resurrect your program back from the dead, which is normally impossible because when a program crashes due to like a seg fault'

[51:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'or whatever, the memory\'s corrupted. It can\'t be recovered, but since we\'re sending that data over to an entirely separate process, it can just bring it back. It\'s so cool. I love it. Um'

[51:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Of course, I hold the philosophy that crash recovery stuff is generally not that useful because ideally your program just shouldn\'t crash. So,'

[51:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'don\'t worry. I\'m not going to be using that as a crutch. It\'s just undo and redo in particular, especially with a program this complicated, it\'s so difficult to get right. Uh'

[52:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a- aside from undo and redo, you\'ll almost never be hitting any crashes. I I consis- um I consistently use this for hours and hours and hours without hitting a single crash. That\'s also largely because I don\'t use undo and redo very often, but if you you'

[52:21] [scene:talk]
